      "Language": "ger",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "An improved opioid-free analgesia concept using thoracic epidural anlgesia with a low concentrated local anaesthetic alone could be of practical interest for hospitals which are unable to guarantee continuous monitoring on the wards."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We have compared in a prospective, randomized, double-blind study, the analgesic effects and side effects of ropivacaine 0.2% (Group 1) with bupivacaine 0.125% plus 0.5 microgram ml-1 sufentanil (Group 2) via a thoracic epidural catheter during the first 24 hours after major abdominal surgery in 28 gynaecological tumour patients. Assessments were performed 8, 12, 16, 20 and 24 hours after surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "No differences were seen in duration of surgery, intraoperative opioid and supplemental analgesic requirements. As a main result VAS values for pain during mobilisation were higher in Group 1 when compared with Group 2 (p < 0.01) after 20 and 24 hours of continuous epidural infusion (10 ml h-1) whereas VAS values at rest were comparable (p < 0.1) at all measurepoints. Intensity of motor block and side effects did not differ between groups."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The present study shows that continuous epidural infusion of 0.2% ropivacaine alone provides comparable analgesia at rest, but less profound pain relief during mobilisation within the first 24 hours after major abdominal surgery than bupivacaine 0.125% with sufentanil."
        }
      ]},
